/` - AST-aware structural search |\n| `actionlint`     | ad hoc workflow review          | `actionlint` - lint GitHub Actions workflows                                        |\n| `zizmor`         | ad hoc workflow security review | `zizmor .github/workflows/` - audit GitHub Actions security posture                 |\n\nUse `ast-grep` for code patterns (avoids false positives from comments/strings). Fall back to `rg` for plain text, docs, and unsupported languages.\n\n## SCM\n\n- Never use `git reset --hard` or push without explicit permission.\n- If force-pushing is required, use `git push --force-with-lease --force-if-includes` (or just `git push --force-with-lease` if previous one is impossible, analyzing the root cause for fail first, and making sure these changes would not override remote branch updates)\n- Never merge PRs without explicit permission — always ask first, even if CI passes.\n- Prefer safe alternatives (`git revert`, new commits, temp branches).\n- If history rewrite seems necessary, explain and ask first.\n- Add `Co-Authored-By: chatgpt-codex-connector[bot] <199175422+chatgpt-codex-connector[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>` to commits by default, unless explicitly asked not to.\n\n## Using GitHub\n\nFor questions about GitHub, use the `gh` tool and `/octocat` skill.\nAlways run `gh` commands with escalated permissions; sandboxed `gh` calls fail in this environment because network access is restricted there.\nNever mention Codex in PR descriptions, PR comments, or issue comments unless asked explicitly.\nDo not include a \"Test plan\" section in PR descriptions.\n\n## Coding Standards\n\n### PHP Coding Standards\n\nWhen working with Laravel/PHP projects, always use the `php-guidelines-from-spatie` skill.\n\n## Reviewing external feedback\n\n- ALWAYS load the `superpowers:receiving-code-review` skill upon receiving review feedback (PR comments, agent reviews). The skill enforces technical rigor and verification over performative agreement.\n- When acting on PR review comments (from bots or humans), independently verify factual claims before applying fixes — especially version numbers, library existence, API behavior, and deprecation status.\n- If a claim falls outside your knowledge cutoff, use WebSearch or check local evidence (installed packages, lock files, docker images) before trusting it.\n- Use `pr-review-fetcher` agent to fetch Pull Review reviews and comments.\n\n## Production safety\n\n- Assume production impact unless stated otherwise.\n- Call out risk when touching auth, billing, data, APIs, or build systems.\n- Prefer small, reversible changes; avoid silent breaking behavior.\n\n## Self improvement\n\n- Continuously improve agent workflows.\n- When a repeated correction or better approach is found you're encouraged to codify your new found knowledge and learnings by modifying your section of `~/.c********S.md`.\n- You can modify `~/.c********S.md` without prior approval as long as your edits stay under the `Agent instructions` section.\n- If you utilize any of your codified instructions in future coding sessions call that out and let the user know that you performed the action because of that specific rule in this file.\n\n## Tool-specific memory\n\n- Actively think beyond the immediate task.\n- When using or working near a tool the user maintains:\n  - If you notice patterns, friction, missing features, risks, or improvement opportunities, jot them down.\n  - Do **not** interrupt the current task to implement speculative changes.\n- Use subagents when they reasonably improve performance: independent research,\n  parallel codebase inspection, adversarial review, or separable implementation\n  work. Do not wait for explicit user permission when the work is non-trivial\n  and the parallelism reduces risk or latency. Keep orchestration lightweight:\n  avoid subagents for tiny, sequential, or highly shared-state edits.\n- In plan mode, always use the `/ask` skill to read the code and the original transcript that generated it. Understanding intent will help you write a better plan.\n- Always start new conversation by loading `/using-superpowers` skill. Do this automatically without being asked.\n  - Ignore `superpowers:using-git-worktrees` skill.\n- Do **not** launch Bash / console commands in the background using `command &` syntax, you have options to run Bash and other tools in background explicitly.\n\n## External File Loading\n\nCRITICAL: When you encounter a file reference (e.g., @rules/general.md), use your Read tool to load it on a need-to-know basis. They're relevant to the SPECIFIC task at hand.\n\nInstructions:\n\n- Do NOT preemptively load all references - use lazy loading based on actual need\n- When loaded, treat content as mandatory instructions that override defaults\n- Follow references recursively when needed\n\n### Authoring CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md\n\n`@file` references in CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md are eagerly injected into agent context on every load by the Claude Code loader, regardless of whether the file is needed for the current task. The lazy-load instructions above govern in-conversation Read behavior; they do NOT override loader-level injection. Authoring choices matter:\n\n- Use `@file` ONLY for small, always-relevant docs (rule of thumb: ≤ ~50 lines and you'd want the content in every conversation in that directory).\n- Use plain markdown links `[file](file)` for everything larger or situational — agents will Read on demand.\n- Authoring heuristic: if you would not paste the file's full contents into every conversation that triggers this CLAUDE.md, do not use `@`.\n- This applies to both `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` (and `.claude.local.md`), and to symlinks between them.\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Agent instructions — llame\n\nHow to work in this repo. [SPEC.md](SPEC.md) is the current cross-cutting architecture contract and documentation index; focused capability behavior lives in OpenSpec. `CLAUDE.md` and `GEMINI.md` are symlinks to this file.\n\nThe product overview (what llame is) is short and always relevant, so it is imported in full:\n\n@README.md\n\n## Key documentation\n\n- [README.md](README.md) — current product overview, shipped baseline, and quickstart (imported above)\n- [VISION.md](VISION.md) — north-star direction, principles, horizons, and deliberate deferrals\n- [ROADMAP.md](ROADMAP.md) — forward-only sequence of unshipped work; GitHub owns live status and implementation detail\n- [SPEC.md](SPEC.md) — current cross-cutting architecture contract, enforced invariants, and authority index\n- [`openspec/specs`](openspec/specs) — normative shipped capability behavior; [`openspec/changes`](openspec/changes) owns proposed deltas and archived implementation records\n- [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md) — shipped chronology\n- [`docs/research`](docs/research) — noncanonical evidence, alternatives, and decision provenance\n- [DESIGN.md](DESIGN.md) — design system reference (visual language, OKLCH tokens, component stylings); consult before building or restyling any UI\n- [docs/scaling.md](docs/scaling.md) — horizontal-scaling topology, invariants, and the design constraints for the durable-run worker split (#48/#50)\n\n## Monorepo layout\n\npnpm + Turborepo workspace, **TypeScript end-to-end** (Node >= 22.12, pinned in `.node-version`; `nix develop` or direnv gives a ready shell; pnpm 10). Workspaces: `apps/*`, `packages/*`.\n\n| Path                         | Role                                                                                        | Stack (details in its own `AGENTS.md`)                                         |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `apps/web`                   | User-facing **thin client** of `apps/api` (auth, chat/project UI); owns no DB               | Next.js 16 (App Router), React 19, TanStack Query, AI SDK (chat transport), ky |\n| `apps/api`                   | API/services + **sole database owner**; ships co-located and dedicated worker entrypoints   | NestJS 11, Drizzle + postgres.js, pg-boss                                      |\n| `apps/storybook`             | Component workbench + browser interaction tests for `packages/ui` stories                   | Storybook 10 (nextjs-vite), Vitest browser mode                                |\n| `packages/ui`                | Shared shadcn/ui component library (`@workspace/ui`); stories co-located next to components | shadcn/ui, Tailwind, React 19                                                  |\n| `packages/config-typescript` | Shared `tsconfig` bases                                                                     | —                                                                              |\n\nEach app/package has its own `AGENTS.md` (auto-loaded when you work in that directory) with concrete commands, structure, and gotchas. **Keep this file high-level — put implementation detail in the child file, not here.**\n\n## Commands (from repo root)\n\n```bash\npnpm install\npnpm dev      # turbo run dev — all apps in watch mode (web + api + storybook on :6006)\npnpm build    # turbo build\npnpm lint     # turbo lint (oxlint per workspace; type-aware via tsgolint in apps/api)\npnpm format   # prettier --write **/*.{ts,tsx,md}   (format:check to verify)\npnpm test:e2e            # playwright test; pass filters after --, e.g. pnpm test:e2e -- e2e/auth\npnpm test:e2e:ui         # playwright test --ui\npnpm test:e2e:headed     # playwright test --headed\npnpm test:e2e:debug      # playwright test --debug\npnpm test:e2e:report     # playwright show-report\n```\n\nScope to one workspace with `pnpm --filter web <script>` (or `--filter api`).\nInstall Playwright browsers once with `pnpm exec playwright install chromium` if the local browser cache is missing. For E2E, Playwright starts a throwaway Docker 